-- title:
--   Regular prorepresentation of the stalk at a closed special point
-- statement:
--   Let $q, q'$ be primes with $q' \neq q$, and let $a, b \in \mathbb{Q}$ be such that $\mathbb{H}[\mathbb{Q}, a, b]$ is indefinite ($0 < a$ or $0 < b$) and, for every height-one prime $v$ of the ring of integers of $\mathbb{Q}$, the algebra $\mathbb{H}[\mathbb{Q}, a, b] \otimes_{\mathbb{Q}} \mathbb{Q}_v$ has all nonzero elements invertible exactly when $v$ contains $q$ or $q'$. Let $\Lambda \subseteq \mathbb{H}[\mathbb{Q}, a, b]$ be a $\mathbb{Z}$-submodule that is an order maximal among orders, and let $m \geq 3$ with $q \nmid m$. Let $M$ be a scheme with a morphism $\pi_M : M \to \operatorname{Spec} \mathbb{Z}_q$, locally of finite type, and let $\mathrm{ptF}$ assign to every commutative ring $S$, every $s : \operatorname{Spec} S \to \operatorname{Spec} \mathbb{Z}_q$, and every pair consisting of a fake elliptic curve over $S$ with $\Lambda$-action and level structure $1$ together with a full level-$m$ structure on it, a morphism $\operatorname{Spec} S \to M$ composing with $\pi_M$ to $s$; assume `IsFineModuli` for these data, that is: $\mathrm{ptF}$ is constant on isomorphism classes, compatible with pullback along ring homomorphisms $S \to S'$, surjective onto the $S$-points of $M$ over $s$, and injective up to isomorphism. Let $y \in M$ be a closed point with $\pi_M(y)$ the closed point of $\operatorname{Spec} \mathbb{Z}_q$. Then there exist a characteristic-zero discrete valuation ring $O^{\mathrm{nr}}$ that is a $\mathbb{Z}_q$-algebra in which the ideal generated by the image of $q$ is maximal; a ring homomorphism $\bar{x}$ from the stalk $\mathcal{O}_{M,y}$ to the residue field of $O^{\mathrm{nr}}$ whose kernel is the maximal ideal of that stalk; and a regular local ring $R$, an $O^{\mathrm{nr}}$-algebra, complete for its maximal-ideal adic topology, together with $\mathrm{res}_R : R \to O^{\mathrm{nr}}/\mathfrak{m}$ satisfying $\mathrm{res}_R \circ (\text{structure map}) = \mathrm{residue}$, and $\varphi : \mathcal{O}_{M,y} \to R$ with $\mathrm{res}_R \circ \varphi = \bar{x}$, such that: for every Artinian local $O^{\mathrm{nr}}$-algebra $A$ with a surjective ring homomorphism $\mathrm{res}_A : A \to O^{\mathrm{nr}}/\mathfrak{m}$ compatible with the residue map of $O^{\mathrm{nr}}$, and every $\psi : \mathcal{O}_{M,y} \to A$ with $\mathrm{res}_A \circ \psi = \bar{x}$, there is a unique $O^{\mathrm{nr}}$-algebra homomorphism $\chi : R \to A$ with $\mathrm{res}_A \circ \chi = \mathrm{res}_R$ and $\chi \circ \varphi = \psi$; and moreover the image of $q$ in $R$ is nonzero.
--
--   This is the local structure statement at the bad prime underlying the Čerednik–Drinfeld description of the fine Shimura curve of a quaternion algebra ramified at $q$: the local ring at a closed point of the special fibre admits a prorepresenting hull that is regular and in which $q$ is not nilpotent, the deformation problem being that of the corresponding special formal $\mathcal{O}_D$-module. It feeds the flatness of $M$ over $\mathbb{Z}_q$ and the nonemptiness of the geometric generic fibre used later in the construction of the curve.
